   <!-- FL resident with Social Security benefits and a rental property in MA. Massachusetts
        does not tax Social Security, so the benefit leaves line 14e and the ratio rises
        above what federal income alone would give (#1201 re-review, follow-up 1).

        Federal (2025), by law: wages 56,000 + rent 30,000 = 86,000 other income;
        benefits $40,000; provisional income = 86,000 + 20,000 = 106,000, above the $34,000
        single threshold, so taxable benefits = min(0.85 x 40,000, 0.85 x (106,000 - 34,000)
        + 4,500) = min(34,000, 65,700) = $34,000; total income (Form 1040 line 9) = $120,000.
        expected_agi is NOT asserted here: Calculate_SS_Taxability puts the whole $40,000
        into gross_income and AGI and never removes the non-taxable $6,000 (a federal defect
        outside #1201), so the rules would compute $126,000 and fail a law-derived figure.
        The Massachusetts figure below does not depend on that defect: line 14e removes
        whatever Social Security the federal total contains, so either way 14e = 56,000.
        If the federal side is corrected and MA_Exemption_Ratio keeps subtracting the whole
        benefit, 14e falls to 50,000, the ratio to 0.3750, and this scenario fails; that is
        what it is for.

        Massachusetts, worked from the 2025 Form 1-NR/PY and its instructions
        (mass.gov/doc/2025-form-1-nrpy-instructions/download):
          line 9/12/14a Massachusetts 5.0% income (the rent)                 $30,000
          line 14d      total income this return                             $30,000
          line 14e      non-Massachusetts source income: total U.S. income
                        less the Social Security it includes, less 14d:
                        120,000 - 34,000 - 30,000                            $56,000
          line 14f      total income: 14d + 14e                              $86,000
          line 14g      deduction and exemption ratio: 30,000 / 86,000      = 0.3488 (four decimal places)
          line 4a/22a   personal exemption, single                           $4,400
          line 22       nonresidents: 22a x 14g = 4,400 x 0.3488            = $1,534.72
          line 23       5.0% income after exemptions: 30,000 - 1,534.72     = $28,465.28
          line 26       tax on 5.0% income: 28,465.28 x 0.05                = $1,423.26 (1,423.264)
        Rent rather than wages for the Massachusetts income so that the line 15 FICA
        deduction, which MA_Tax does not model, does not enter the figure. -->
   <id>MS-19-FL-MA-RENTAL-SOCIAL-SECURITY</id>
   <tax_year>2025</tax_year>
   <filing_status>Single</filing_status>
   <state>FL</state>

   <expected_ma_nonresident_tax>1423.26</expected_ma_nonresident_tax>

   <state_periods>
      <state_period id="1">
         <id>1</id>
         <state_code>FL</state_code>
         <start_date>1/1/2025</start_date>
         <end_date>12/31/2025</end_date>
         <resident_status>full_year</resident_status>
      </state_period>
      <state_period id="2">
         <id>2</id>
         <state_code>MA</state_code>
         <start_date>1/1/2025</start_date>
         <end_date>12/31/2025</end_date>
         <resident_status>nonresident</resident_status>
      </state_period>
   </state_periods>

   <taxpayers>
      <taxpayer id="1">
         <id>1</id>
         <name>Test Taxpayer</name>
         <date_of_birth>8/30/1961</date_of_birth>
         <is_primary>true</is_primary>
         <w2_wages>56000</w2_wages>
         <social_security_income>40000</social_security_income>
      </taxpayer>
   </taxpayers>

   <incomes>
      <income id="1">
         <id>1</id>
         <taxpayer_id>1</taxpayer_id>
         <type>w2_wages</type>
         <source>Employer in FL</source>
         <gross_amount>56000</gross_amount>
         <state_code>FL</state_code>
      </income>
      <income id="2">
         <id>2</id>
         <taxpayer_id>1</taxpayer_id>
         <type>rental</type>
         <source>Rental property in MA</source>
         <gross_amount>30000</gross_amount>
         <state_code>MA</state_code>
      </income>
   </incomes>

   <dependents></dependents>
   <properties></properties>
   <vehicles></vehicles>
   <deductions></deductions>
